# Security design

## Scope and evidence
State inspected paths and classification source.

## Assets, actors and trust boundaries
Identify valuable data/actions, principals, external services, untrusted input points, tenants and trust transitions from observed evidence.

## Threat model
Describe project-specific attack paths such as broken object authorization, account abuse, scraping, resource exhaustion, injection, SSRF, credential exposure, and supply-chain risks where relevant.

## Identity and authorization
Define evidenced authentication and server-side per-action/per-object authorization; mark missing roles, tenant boundaries, sessions or ownership rules unresolved.

## Bots, abuse and resource limits
Specify evidence-backed rate/quotas, account/source limits, body/concurrency/queue/cost bounds, safe limit responses, monitoring and abuse tests. Do not invent numeric thresholds.

## Agentic AI and tool safeguards
If applicable, cover untrusted prompt/retrieval/tool content, least privilege, scoped identities, allowlisted validated tools, output checks, human approval for consequential actions, sandbox/egress limits, budgets and adversarial tests. Otherwise state technical N/A.

## Data, secrets and operations
Document observed validation, encoding, storage/retention, secrets/key lifecycle, encryption, logging/redaction, dependencies, monitoring and recovery controls. Separate implemented from Proposed controls.

## Verification and incident response
List actual commands/tests and results, planned security verification, monitoring/response ownership and recovery steps; mark unknowns unresolved.

## Open risks and response
Prioritize residual risks, missing evidence/decisions, affected release boundary and next safe action. Never claim “unhackable” or certified security.
